      <h2>Radon in Western Washington: Geology &amp; EPA Zone 3</h2>
      <p>
        Radon is an odorless, colorless radioactive gas formed by the natural decay of trace uranium in deep rock and soil. Because it creeps upward through soil, it can enter homes through foundation cracks, sump pumps, or crawl spaces.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Washington State, elevated radon is heavily concentrated in Eastern Washington (Spokane, Stevens, and Ferry counties) due to granite-rich geology. By contrast, the Puget Sound basin consists primarily of marine clay and glaciomarine drift. As a result, the EPA designates both King and Snohomish counties as <strong>Zone 3 (Predicted average indoor radon screening level below 2.0 pCi/L)</strong>, far below the federal action threshold of <strong>4.0 pCi/L</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>When Does Radon Testing Make Sense?</h2>
      <p>
        While general risk across the Puget Sound region is low, localized soil pockets or specific home designs can occasionally trap soil gas:
      </p>

      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property Scenario</th>
            <th>Local Risk Level</th>
            <th>Recommended Action</th>
          </tr>
