      <h2>Free State Test Kits vs. Commercial Continuous Monitors</h2>
      <p>
        If you want total peace of mind without paying commercial inspector fees ($150–$300), Washington State provides accessible testing options:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Free Test Kits from WA Dept of Health:</strong> The Washington State Department of Health (DOH) Radon Program offers free DIY short-term radon test kits to state residents. You simply fill out an online request form at <code>doh.wa.gov</code>, place the canister in your primary living space for 3 to 7 days, and mail it to the lab with pre-paid postage.</li>
        <li><strong>Hardware Store Kits ($15–$25):</strong> DIY test kits are also available at local hardware stores (Home Depot, Lowe's) for quick turnaround.</li>
        <li><strong>Sub-Slab Depressurization Fixes:</strong> If a test ever reveals radon above 4.0 pCi/L, installing an active sub-slab depressurization system (a PVC vent pipe and small inline exhaust fan) typically costs $1,500–$2,500 and permanently reduces radon to near zero.</li>
      </ul>
